
1. Two spellings, and zsh takes both
--------------------------------------------------------------
trap 'echo "trap INT   -- not stopping" >> log' INT     # bash's spelling
TRAPQUIT() { echo "TRAPQUIT   -- not stopping" >> log }  # zsh's own
trap 'echo "trap KILL"                  >> log' KILL

zsh accepted all three without a word, including the last. Sending the
first two:

$ cat log
trap INT   -- not stopping
TRAPQUIT   -- not stopping

A function named TRAPINT, TRAPQUIT or TRAPTERM is a handler, with no
trap command anywhere. It is the readable form when the handler is more
than one line, and it is zsh-only -- bash reads it as a plain function.

2. The third one, which no spelling reaches
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ cat log
trap INT   -- not stopping
TRAPQUIT   -- not stopping

$ wait $victim; echo $?
137

Two lines still, and 128 + 9 = 137. SIGKILL and SIGSTOP are removed by
the kernel before any process sees them, so there is no shell, no
handler and no spelling that changes this.

3. kill -l, which zsh prints differently from bash
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ kill -l INT
2

$ kill -l STOP
17

$ kill -l
HUP INT QUIT ILL TRAP ABRT EMT FPE KILL BUS SEGV SYS PIPE ALRM TERM URG STOP TSTP CONT CHLD TTIN TTOU IO XCPU XFSZ VTALRM PROF WINCH INFO USR1 USR2

zsh's bare 'kill -l' is a list of names and nothing else; bash prints
numbered pairs. Reading a number out of either is the habit to lose --
SIGSTOP is 17 on macOS and 19 on Linux, as the two columns here show.

4. The one thing zsh will not let a script do
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ zsh -c "setopt monitor"
zsh:setopt:1: can't change option: monitor

bash turns job control on inside a script with 'set -m'; zsh refuses in
a non-interactive shell and says so. Worth knowing before you copy a
bash script's 'set -m' across and wonder why the job is still in the
same process group.

5. A background job's SIGINT was never yours to catch
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ cat log
catcher: caught SIGINT

It caught it -- and the bash column of this page did not. Both shells
were handed the same thing: a parent starting a background job sets the
child's SIGINT and SIGQUIT to ignored before exec, whichever shell the
parent is. POSIX then says a signal already ignored at startup cannot be
trapped, reset or caught. bash honours that rule; zsh overrides it and
installs the trap anyway, and so does fish.

Measured every way round: the parent shell makes no difference and the
child shell makes all of it. A backgrounded wrapper whose Ctrl-C handler
works is a wrapper running under zsh or fish, and changing its shebang
to bash is enough to lose the handler with nothing printed about it.
